##########RHEL 9 & RHEL 10 — Hostname Configuration#############

A hostname is the name assigned to a Linux system. 
It is used to identify a server and can appear in the shell prompt, logs, monitoring systems, and network configuration.
In RHEL 9 and RHEL 10, hostname management is primarily handled by systemd and the hostnamectl command.

1. Introduction

A hostname is the name assigned to a Linux system. It is used to identify a server 
and can appear in the shell prompt, logs, monitoring systems, and network configuration.

In RHEL 9 and RHEL 10, hostname management is primarily handled by systemd and the hostnamectl command.

  
2. Where is the hostname stored?
  
 The persistent static hostname is stored in:

  /etc/hostname

You can view it using: #cat /etc/hostname

3. Check the Current Hostname Using hostname

#hostname

Example output: server01.example.com

Using hostnamectl:

#hostnamectl or #hostnamectl status
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
Static hostname: server01.example.com
       Icon name: computer-vm
         Chassis: vm
      Machine ID: xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
         Boot ID: xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
  Virtualization: kvm
Operating System: Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9
     CPE OS Name: cpe:/o:redhat:enterprise_linux:9
          Kernel: Linux 5.x.x
    Architecture: x86-64
-----------------------------------------------------------------


Important:

hostnamectl set-hostname updates the hostname for the currently running system 
and also makes the configuration persistent. Therefore, normally a reboot is not required.

  5. Manually Editing /etc/hostname

  You can manually edit: #sudo vi /etc/hostname

For example, change the contents to: server01.example.com

Then check:  #cat /etc/hostname




